From: OpenX6X00 <noreply@rf.guru>
Subject: [PATCH] message: a standard callsign is what WSJT-X takes for one (chkcall.f90)

WSJT-X takes a callsign for a standard one (28 bits in a type 1 message,
with a grid) only when one of its first two characters is a letter, a
digit comes second or third, one to three letters follow, it has at most
six characters, and it does not start with Q (chkcall.f90: "Real calls
don't start with Q"). Others it sends as nonstandard calls (type 4), and
a type 1 message with one of them in it is thrown away by its decoder.

ft8_lib packed more as standard: Q calls (the bench's Q1TST, Q2TST), and
through the old 3X -> Q and 3DA0 -> 3D0 shortcuts Guinea's 3XA1AB and
Eswatini's 3DA0XY. WSJT-X's jt9 decoded none of them, from ft8_lib's own
clean signal or off the air, while ft8_lib decoded them (2026-11-21: a
radio's FT8 app sending CQ Q1TST, heard at -21 dBFS in the other radio's
TCI audio, decoded by its FT8 app, never by WSJT-X). Even WSJT-X's own
exception, QU1RK, comes back as 3XU1RK (its ft8code: "bad message"), so
no Q call is standard here.

Now they go as nonstandard calls, as WSJT-X sends them, and jt9 and
ft8_lib read the same text from 15 test messages (standard, Q, 3X, 3DA0,
3D2, A61, 4U1, E51, 2E0, N0CALL, /P, reports, RR73). The decode side's
3X and 3DA0 mappings stay, for senders that still use them. The tests'
standard-message list drops the 3DA0 and 3X calls and checks them, with
Q1TST and QU1RK, as nonstandard messages: 2617 OK, 0 failed.
---
--- a/ft8/message.c
+++ b/ft8/message.c
@@ -670,39 +670,50 @@
     return found;
 }
 
+/// Whether WSJT-X takes the callsign for a standard one (its chkcall.f90): one of the first two characters a letter,
+/// a digit second or third, then one to three letters, six characters at most, and not starting with Q ("Real calls
+/// don't start with Q"). A call ft8_lib packed as standard but WSJT-X does not (Q1TST; Guinea's 3XA1AB and
+/// Eswatini's 3DA0XY, which the old 3X -> Q and 3DA0 -> 3D0 shortcuts packed) was thrown away by WSJT-X's decoder;
+/// WSJT-X sends them as nonstandard calls, and so does this now, so both read each other's. Even WSJT-X's own
+/// exception, QU1RK, came back as 3XU1RK (its ft8code: "bad message"), so no Q call is standard here
+static bool wsjtx_standard_call(const char* c, int n)
+{
+    if (n < 3 || n > 6)
+        return false;
+    if (!is_letter(c[0]) && !is_letter(c[1]))
+        return false;
+    if (c[0] == 'Q')
+        return false;
+    int i1 = -1;
+    if (is_digit(c[1]))
+        i1 = 1;
+    if (is_digit(c[2]))
+        i1 = 2;
+    if (i1 < 0 || n - i1 - 1 < 1 || n - i1 - 1 > 3)
+        return false;
+    for (int i = i1 + 1; i < n; ++i)
+        if (!is_letter(c[i]))
+            return false;
+    return true;
+}
+
 static int32_t pack_basecall(const char* callsign, int length)
 {
-    if (length > 2)
+    if (wsjtx_standard_call(callsign, length))
     {
         // Attempt to pack a standard callsign, if fail, revert to hashed callsign
         char c6[6] = { ' ', ' ', ' ', ' ', ' ', ' ' };
 
-        // Copy callsign to 6 character buffer
-        if (starts_with(callsign, "3DA0") && (length > 4) && (length <= 7))
+        // Check the position of callsign digit and make a right-aligned copy into c6
+        if (is_digit(callsign[2]) && length <= 6)
         {
-            // Work-around for Swaziland prefix: 3DA0XYZ -> 3D0XYZ
-            memcpy(c6, "3D0", 3);
-            memcpy(c6 + 3, callsign + 4, length - 4);
+            // AB0XYZ
+            memcpy(c6, callsign, length);
         }
-        else if (starts_with(callsign, "3X") && is_letter(callsign[2]) && length <= 7)
+        else if (is_digit(callsign[1]) && length <= 5)
         {
-            // Work-around for Guinea prefixes: 3XA0XYZ -> QA0XYZ
-            memcpy(c6, "Q", 1);
-            memcpy(c6 + 1, callsign + 2, length - 2);
-        }
-        else
-        {
-            // Check the position of callsign digit and make a right-aligned copy into c6
-            if (is_digit(callsign[2]) && length <= 6)
-            {
-                // AB0XYZ
-                memcpy(c6, callsign, length);
-            }
-            else if (is_digit(callsign[1]) && length <= 5)
-            {
-                // A0XYZ -> " A0XYZ"
-                memcpy(c6 + 1, callsign, length);
-            }
+            // A0XYZ -> " A0XYZ"
+            memcpy(c6 + 1, callsign, length);
         }
 
         // Check for standard callsign
